 **Parameters:**
-- **Focus Point:** User-defined. No typical values given [S1, S2].
-- **Initial Brick Size:** Specified in advance. No typical values given [S2].
+*   **Focus Point ($P_f$):** The user-defined point in space that determines the area of highest interest [S1, S2].
+*   **Initial Brick Size:** The physical dimension of the finest-level bricks, specified in advance [S2].
 
 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- **Approximation Error:** The method does not consider approximation error introduced by sampling data at a certain resolution level [S1, S2].
-- **High Frequencies:** If high frequencies are present in the data, the approximation may differ significantly from the original signal, causing artifacts between brick boundaries even if continuity is guaranteed [S1, S2].
-- **Non-Topology Preserving:** Structures may disappear due to low-pass filtering during coarsification, leading to visual artifacts at brick boundaries if the resampled approximation differs significantly from the original data [S1].
-- **Level Transitions:** The method allows arbitrary texture resolutions per brick, provided level transitions greater than one are not specified [S1, S2].
+*   **Approximation Error:** The method does not account for approximation error. High-frequency data sampled at a lower resolution can deviate significantly from the original signal [S1, S2].
+*   **Topological Artifacts:** Non-topology preserving coarsification can cause structures to disappear due to low-pass filtering, leading to visual artifacts at brick boundaries [S1].
+*   **Boundary Discontinuities:** Even if continuity is guaranteed by boundary adaptation, artifacts may still occur if the resampled approximation differs significantly from the original data [S1, S2].
